{"name":"Plantation","hex":"#3e594c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Plantation","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city in Broward County, Florida, named after the Everglades Plantation Co.","A census-designated place in Sarasota County, Florida."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Plantation"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plantation","blocks":[{"text":"Plantations are farms specializing in cash crops, usually mainly planting a single crop, with perhaps ancillary areas for vegetables for eating and so on. Plantations, centered on a plantation house, grow crops including cotton, cannabis, tobacco, coffee, tea, cocoa, sugar cane, opium, coca, sisal, oil seeds, oil palms, fruits, rubber trees and forest trees. Plantation crops are grown on large lands called estates. These crops are grown with scientific methods and a large, highly skilled labor force. Crops such as tea and coffee are grown on hillslopes as they cannot withstand stagnant water. Protectionist policies and natural comparative advantage have sometimes contributed to determining where plantations are located."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Los-esclavos-en-cuba-1.jpg/330px-Los-esclavos-en-cuba-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plantation","wikidata":"Q188913"},{"title":"Plantation (settlement or colony)","blocks":[{"text":"In the history of colonialism, a plantation was a form of colonization in which settlers would establish permanent or semi-permanent colonial settlements in a new region. The term first appeared in the 1580s in the English language to describe the process of colonization before being also used to refer to a colony by the 1610s. By the 1710s, the word was also being used to describe large farms where cash crop goods were produced, typically in tropical regions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Providence_1650_by_Jean_Blackburn.jpg/330px-Providence_1650_by_Jean_Blackburn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plantation_(settlement_or_colony)","wikidata":"Q7201599"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Sugar_cane_workers_resting_1a34016v.jpg/500px-Sugar_cane_workers_resting_1a34016v.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Sugar_cane_workers_resting_1a34016v.jpg/500px-Sugar_cane_workers_resting_1a34016v.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sugar cane workers in Puerto Rico, 1941","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASugar_cane_workers_resting_1a34016v.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Cuba_canna_da_zucchero.jpg/500px-Cuba_canna_da_zucchero.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Cuba_canna_da_zucchero.jpg/1280px-Cuba_canna_da_zucchero.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Cuba_canna_da_zucchero.jpg/500px-Cuba_canna_da_zucchero.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sugarcane plantation in rural Cuba","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACuba_canna_da_zucchero.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Harvesting_tea_in_Bogor%2C_West_Java.jpg/500px-Harvesting_tea_in_Bogor%2C_West_Java.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Harvesting_tea_in_Bogor%2C_West_Java.jpg/500px-Harvesting_tea_in_Bogor%2C_West_Java.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumb